# Reference

## Commands at a glance

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `hql eval <program>` | check and evaluate a program given as an argument |
| `hql run <file>` | check and evaluate a program from a file |
| `hql check <file>` | check a program and print its type, without evaluating it |
| `hql repl` | read, evaluate and print, one input at a time, keeping bindings |
| `hql plan <file>` | print each pipeline's steps and the stage the planner gives each, without evaluating |
| `hql boundary <file> <id>` | print a configured endpoint's boundary: each field, its external name and type, and where each setting came from |
| `hql render <file>…` | run the HQL cells in a document and write each cell's output |
| `hql help <name>` | the declarations of a function, type, trait or module, with their doc strings |
| `hql builtins` | list the steps a pipeline can use |
| `hql config` | the reporting settings in force and where each came from; list, show and select named contexts |
| `hql context [file]` | the execution context: the mode, the project, the vault, and where each came from |
| `hql doctor` | check the semantic-search chain and name the first missing part |
| `hql package <command>` | install, search, update, list, rebuild and remove packages; [chapter 36](../book/36-installing-packages/index.hmd) |

`hql '<program>'` is `hql eval '<program>'`. `-` reads standard
input wherever a file is taken. Success is exit `0`, a
language or I/O failure `1`, a usage error `2`.

## Doc strings

A block of `///` lines opened after an empty line is the doc string of the
declarations below it, up to the next empty line. `hql help <name>` and
`:help <name>` in `hql repl` print each declaration of a name with its module
and doc string; `:type <name>` prints the first sentence. The pages below are
the standard library's doc strings, one page per function, type and trait.

<!-- hql reference: index -->
## The standard library

- [`std/core`](std/core/index.hmd) — The types every other module is written in: the scalars, the collection types, the open tree `Data`, absence, failure, value order, the format category and the core traits.
- [`std/doc`](std/doc/index.hmd) — Documents: a header of everything known about one and a body someone wrote, and the card, a document a vault holds.
- [`std/retrieval`](std/retrieval/index.hmd) — Retrieval: a corpus ranked against a query.
- [`std/graph`](std/graph/index.hmd) — Graphs: nodes and the edges among them, structural only; what a node or an edge means is the `knowledge` module's.
- [`std/knowledge`](std/knowledge/index.hmd) — Knowledge: the semantic domain, where everything is a claim.
- [`std/collections`](std/collections/index.hmd) — The operations over the collection types `core` declares: membership, filtering, mapping, order, grouping, joins and reductions.
- [`std/string`](std/string/index.hmd) — The functions of a `String`, in the prelude.
- [`std/seq`](std/seq/index.hmd) — The sequence, set and map operations beyond the core's membership, order and lookup, in the prelude.
- [`std/math`](std/math/index.hmd) — Numbers, in the prelude.
- [`std/stats`](std/stats/index.hmd) — Descriptive statistics over a collection of numbers: the mean, the sample and population variance and standard deviation, the median, quantiles, the mode, covariance and correlation.
- [`std/time`](std/time/index.hmd) — Time: zones, the units a value truncates to, and the operations over the time scalars of `core`: conversion from text, parts, arithmetic, truncation, rendering, and the evaluation clock.
- [`std/random`](std/random/index.hmd) — Randomness: samples, shuffles, choices, numbers and UUIDs.
- [`std/option`](std/option/index.hmd) — The ways out of an `Option<T>`, in the prelude, each stating what absence gives.
- [`std/result`](std/result/index.hmd) — The ways out of a `Result<T, E>`, in the prelude, each stating what a failure gives.
- [`std/regex`](std/regex/index.hmd) — Text matched against a regular expression.
- [`std/format`](std/format/index.hmd) — The formats, how bytes are read as values and how values are written as text, and the codec contracts over them.
- [`std/io`](std/io/index.hmd) — Input and output: endpoints, the data sources read from them, and the data targets written to them.
- [`std/io/fs`](std/io/fs/index.hmd) — The file system as data: folders, globs over paths, and the entries a folder holds, with their kinds, sizes and modification times.
- [`std/io/env`](std/io/env/index.hmd) — What a program reads from the invocation that runs it: environment variables and named arguments.
- [`std/io/net`](std/io/net/index.hmd) — Network endpoints: transports, read and written by `source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink` like any other endpoint (module `io`).
- [`std/io/logging`](std/io/logging/index.hmd) — Logging: structured events written to a destination, apart from the result.
- [`std/io/db/sqlite`](std/io/db/sqlite/index.hmd) — SQLite: one table of a database file, as a transport read by `source` and `tap` and written by `target` like any other endpoint (module `io`).
- [`std/io/db/postgres`](std/io/db/postgres/index.hmd) — PostgreSQL: one table of a database, as a transport read by `source` and `tap` and written by `target` like any other endpoint (module `io`).
- [`std/io/db/spark`](std/io/db/spark/index.hmd) — Spark SQL: one table behind a Spark SQL endpoint, a Databricks SQL warehouse or a Spark Thrift Server, as a transport read by `source` and `tap` and written by `target` like any other endpoint (module `io`).
- [`std/io/transaction`](std/io/transaction/index.hmd) — The control calls of a transaction scope's handle, `tx`, bound by `with transaction tx on participants { … }` (module `io`): each ends the attempt and gives no value, so it stands where any type is expected and nothing after it runs.
- [`std/io/msg/nats`](std/io/msg/nats/index.hmd) — Core NATS: one subject, as a transport read by `tap` and written by `sink` and `target` like any other endpoint (module `io`).
- [`std/io/msg/nats_jetstream`](std/io/msg/nats_jetstream/index.hmd) — NATS JetStream: one subject of a stream, as a transport read by `tap` and written by `sink` and `target` like any other endpoint (module `io`).
- [`std/crypto/digest`](std/crypto/digest/index.hmd) — Cryptographic digests of text and of `Data` trees.
- [`std/encoding`](std/encoding/index.hmd) — Text-safe encodings of a text's UTF-8 octets.
- [`std/bytes`](std/bytes/index.hmd) — The functions of a `Bytes` value, in the prelude.
- [`std/crypto`](std/crypto/index.hmd) — Keys, signatures, MACs, password hashes and encryption.
- [`hmd`](hmd/index.hmd) — HyperMarkdown in HQL: the builtin package `hmd`, beside `std`.
- [`hmd/vault`](hmd/vault/index.hmd) — The current vault: the HyperMarkdown vault a program reads (HQL-0059).
- [`hmd/search`](hmd/search/index.hmd) — Retrieval over a vault's cards: `hmd.search.lexical` ranks by the words a card shares with a query, `hmd.search.semantic` by vectors from the vault's index.
- [`hmd/search/lexical`](hmd/search/lexical/index.hmd) — Lexical retrieval: a corpus ranked by the words it shares with a query, a hashed bag of words compared by cosine.
- [`hmd/search/semantic`](hmd/search/semantic/index.hmd) — Semantic retrieval: a corpus ranked against a query by vectors a language model made ahead of time, read from the index that `[semantics] index` in the vault's `.hmd/config.toml` names.
- [`std/validators`](std/validators/index.hmd) — Validators: the standard validator traits, imported where they are used, `import validators`, and never by the prelude, so a program's own `Email` is its own.
<!-- hql reference: end -->
